  "details": "<p>The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: at Yaz\u0131l\u0131kaya</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "A plateau around a monumental Phrygian rock\u2010cut altar at the extensive fortified settlement and sanctuary complex of Yaz\u0131l\u0131kaya in Turkey. Associated with Agdistis in the 20th century because of Roman epigraphic finds there, more recent excavations have demonstrated that the sanctuary was in use from the 8th\u20137th century BCE to the Roman Period (1st-2nd century CE). Continuity of ritual practice in front of the rock-cut altar is demonstrated by rock-cut bows, a ritual preparation area with hearths, a rock-cut statue identifiable as the goddess Matar, and the Roman-era votive inscriptions.",
